 and it's very difficult to monetize custody.

153
00:06:40,560 --> 00:06:43,640
 Unless you have some of these additional services on top,

154
00:06:43,640 --> 00:06:46,000
 what do you think is the future of profitability

155
00:06:46,000 --> 00:06:47,000
 of crypto custodians?

156
00:06:47,000 --> 00:06:48,640
 I mean, do you think they will need to have these services

157
00:06:48,640 --> 00:06:50,680
 on top and slurry services,

158
00:06:50,680 --> 00:06:52,880
 or that it's gonna be custody by itself

159
00:06:52,880 --> 00:06:55,760
 is a business model that has a lot of future?

160
00:06:55,760 --> 00:06:57,600
 - All right, so there's a couple of points here, right?

161
00:06:57,600 --> 00:07:02,000
 So first of all, the commoditization of custody.

162
00:07:02,000 --> 00:07:04,520
 So it is true that custody is a commodity

163
00:07:04,520 --> 00:07:06,160
 in the traditional space,

164
00:07:06,160 --> 00:07:09,800
 but in the crypto space, we're far from it, in my opinion.

165
00:07:09,800 --> 00:07:13,840
 So the technology is evolving so fast.

166
00:07:13,840 --> 00:07:15,640
 We have new blockchain protocols,

167
00:07:15,640 --> 00:07:19,040
 we have new use cases, we have new business models,

168
00:07:19,040 --> 00:07:22,240
 and custody is really at the core

169
00:07:22,240 --> 00:07:25,520
 of facilitating and enabling these business models.

170
00:07:25,520 --> 00:07:29,200
 So whatever custodians were doing three years ago,

171
00:07:29,200 --> 00:07:32,520
 it is very different from what we are doing today.

172
00:07:32,520 --> 00:07:35,520
 So I would say that, yes, probably at a certain point

173
00:07:35,520 --> 00:07:39,440
 in the future, we'll have a more standard offering

174
00:07:39,440 --> 00:07:42,120
 of custody, but as of today,

175
00:07:42,120 --> 00:07:43,880
 from a technological perspective,

176
00:07:43,880 --> 00:07:46,040
 we're really far from having,

177
00:07:46,040 --> 00:07:48,520
 from custody being a commodity.

178
00:07:48,520 --> 00:07:52,240
 There's also a couple of aspects that are relevant

179
00:07:52,240 --> 00:07:56,160
 to the how custody is evolving in the blockchain space.

180
00:07:56,160 --> 00:08:00,160
 So the first one is, obviously that in the traditional space,

181
00:08:00,160 --> 00:08:03,080
 custody is basically a simple ledger

182
00:08:03,080 --> 00:08:06,680
 where you record who owns what, right?

183
00:08:06,680 --> 00:08:09,880
 And then you transfer, I mean, you synchronize this ledger

184
00:08:09,880 --> 00:08:13,280
 with the central depository institutions, et cetera.

185
00:08:13,280 --> 00:08:15,440
 So assets in the traditional space,

186
00:08:15,440 --> 00:08:18,920
 they're pure ledger entries.

187
00:08:18,920 --> 00:08:23,920
 And the blockchain space assets are basically smart contracts.

188
00:08:23,920 --> 00:08:27,240
 It means that they are programmable, they're live animals,

189
00:08:27,240 --> 00:08:29,680
 and some of the financial services

190
00:08:29,680 --> 00:08:33,680
 are already enabled within the asset.

191
00:08:33,680 --> 00:08:36,720
 So the asset is not a simple entry,

192
00:08:36,720 --> 00:08:39,680
 but it has already embedded in the asset itself

193
00:08:39,680 --> 00:08:41,400
 a number of services.

194
00:08:41,400 --> 00:08:43,960
 And these services, sometimes they don't require

195
00:08:43,960 --> 00:08:46,920
 an intermediary to enable them.

196
00:08:46,920 --> 00:08:49,320
 They require the custodian to make sure

197
00:08:49,320 --> 00:08:52,240
 that these assets can be useful for those services.

198
00:08:52,240 --> 00:08:55,040
 I'm talking, for example, about staking, right?

199
00:08:55,040 --> 00:08:58,920
 You don't need anymore an intermediary to enable staking,

200
00:08:58,920 --> 00:08:59,760
 right?

201
00:08:59,760 --> 00:09:01,840
 You can do it directly from your custodian

202
00:09:01,840 --> 00:09:04,680
 by signing a staking transaction on your assets,

203
00:09:04,680 --> 00:09:07,160
 because that staking transaction is part of the protocol

204
00:09:07,160 --> 00:09:09,600
 where the asset has been issued.

205
00:09:09,600 --> 00:09:11,760
 So there's a very important difference

206
00:09:11,760 --> 00:09:13,640
 from the traditional space,

207
00:09:13,640 --> 00:09:17,560
 and it has an impact on how commoditized the service is

208
00:09:17,560 --> 00:09:19,240
 and how you can monetize it.

209
00:09:19,240 --> 00:09:21,160
 Now, going to the monetization, yes,

210
00:09:21,160 --> 00:09:23,400
 obviously monetization is extremely complex,

211
00:09:23,400 --> 00:09:26,320
 and I would agree with you that it's much harder

212
00:09:26,320 --> 00:09:28,360
 to monetize a custody service

213
00:09:28,360 --> 00:09:32,480
 versus a trading service or an exchange service, et cetera.

214
00:09:32,480 --> 00:09:36,280
 However, the custody business brings

215
00:09:36,280 --> 00:09:37,760
 a couple of advantages, right?

216
00:09:37,760 --> 00:09:41,720
 So the first one is that it's a very staking business, right?

217
00:09:41,720 --> 00:09:46,720
 Which allows you to basically create some kind of barriers

218
00:09:46,720 --> 00:09:51,960
 of entry or switching costs for your clients,

219
00:09:51,960 --> 00:09:54,280
 and allows you to monetize your clients

220
00:09:54,280 --> 00:09:57,200
 more in the long term versus other services.

221
00:09:57,200 --> 00:09:59,520
 And at the same time, you're exactly right.

222
00:09:59,520 --> 00:10:02,160
 So there are certain services that are enabled

223
00:10:02,160 --> 00:10:03,880
 by custodians, right?

224
00:10:03,880 --> 00:10:06,840
 And those services are much easier to monetize

225
00:10:06,840 --> 00:10:08,720
 than the pure custody service.

226
00:10:08,720 --> 00:10:10,360
 If we think about staking,

227
00:10:10,360 --> 00:10:13,360
 is one of the most profitable activities in today's market,

228
00:10:13,360 --> 00:10:16,440
 and custodians really have the capability

229
00:10:16,440 --> 00:10:20,600
 of capturing most of the monetization opportunity.

230
00:10:20,600 --> 00:10:21,920
 - So let's talk about staking.

231
00:10:21,920 --> 00:10:23,040
 I mean, you know, we're seeing now

232
00:10:23,040 --> 00:10:24,880
 a rise of staking as a service, right?

233
00:10:24,880 --> 00:10:27,240
 Where a lot of custodians are offering the service,

234
00:10:27,240 --> 00:10:29,080
 even a lot of crypto exchanges are offering,

235
00:10:29,080 --> 00:10:31,360
 you know, staking services as well.

236
00:10:31,360 --> 00:10:34,360
 And I mean, for many holders of certain crypto assets,

237
00:10:34,360 --> 00:10:37,800
 whether it's Ethereum or other layer ones or layer twos,

238
00:10:37,800 --> 00:10:39,440
 staking is actually pretty good way

239
00:10:39,440 --> 00:10:41,240
 to get a yield on that asset.

240
00:10:41,240 --> 00:10:43,840
 What's your view on staking as a service?

241
00:10:43,840 --> 00:10:45,520
 Do you think in the next couple of years,

242
00:10:45,520 --> 00:10:48,960
 we're gonna see all the custodians offer staking services?

243
00:10:48,960 --> 00:10:52,080
 And how difficult you think that is

244
00:10:52,080 --> 00:10:53,880
 for a custodian to set that up?

245
00:10:54,840 --> 00:10:57,240
 All right, so staking is, I mean,

246
00:10:57,240 --> 00:10:59,600
 as of today in the blockchain space,

247
00:10:59,600 --> 00:11:03,680
 obviously proof of stake is the state of art, right?

248
00:11:03,680 --> 00:11:06,440
 So it is obvious that staking is kind of the,

249
00:11:06,440 --> 00:11:09,360
 this inflation protection mechanism, right?

250
00:11:09,360 --> 00:11:12,360
 Similar to the overnight rate that we have

251
00:11:12,360 --> 00:11:14,520
 in the traditional market, right?

252
00:11:14,520 --> 00:11:18,720
 So we'll, eventually we'll have a kind of a staking yield

253
00:11:18,720 --> 00:11:21,120
 that will be more or less aligned with the inflation

254
00:11:21,120 --> 00:11:23,080
 that the protocol offers.

255
00:11:23,080 --> 00:11:27,440
 So if the investor wants to protect himself against

256
00:11:27,440 --> 00:11:31,640
 the inflation of the protocol, staking is the solution.

257
00:11:31,640 --> 00:11:34,560
 So it is something that is here at the state

258
00:11:34,560 --> 00:11:37,320
 until proof of stake would be the state of the art

259
00:11:37,320 --> 00:11:39,760
 for blockchain protocols.

260
00:11:39,760 --> 00:11:43,280
 Now, since you mentioned exchanges,

261
00:11:43,280 --> 00:11:47,600
 I think that it's very important to do a clarification

262
00:11:47,600 --> 00:11:51,520
 between what staking is on an exchange platform,

263
00:11:51,520 --> 00:11:53,400
 a centralized exchange platform,

264
